Am 14.09.2012 21:32, schrieb Michael Turniansky:
> Just a minor grammatical point about your letter, van.. You cannot=20
> cluster a cmavo together with another if it starts with a vowel (as=20
> you have done below with *"i.iua" and *".iuisai" without putting a=20
> pause (period or space in written lojban) before it. So that should be=20
> either ".i uisai" or ".i.uisai"

Without wanting to re-open this debate from a while ago, I still don't=20
like seeing this kind of information being spread. Neither a pause nor a=20
period nor a space is needed in written or spoken Lojban. ".iua"=20
unambiguously fragments into .i ua. I'd repeat myself and say that ua is=20
a CV, but I don't want to get falsely flamed again for saying the truth.=20
For completeness sake however, I should add that there are no words that=20
begin with a (phonetic) vowel in Lojban, so the rule you formulated=20
above just never applies. What I'm saying might seem wrong or alien to=20
you, but I've done enough research and have thought about this topic at=20
length. You are free to reject it, of course. If you'd like to discuss=20
this in more depth (since it probably makes no sense to you here without=20
the proper context), feel free to open a new thread.
I'd like to mention that because you are by far not the only one who=20
gets confused about Lojban's "denpa bu", I created a slightly altered=20
way of writing lojban some time ago which makes it impossible to=20
mispronounce things and which always clearly indicates what's going on=20
phonologically/morphologically (the goal being actual full isomorphism=20
within the limits of a simple latin alphabet using only a few=20
diacritics). Some people like it, some don't. Some like it but don't use=20
it, others actually have adopted it. It does help people though to not=20
be confused about Lojban's phonology.
Bottomline, you really don't need a "denpa bu" in the sentence in question.

mu'o mi'e la selpa'i

